01:42:00,191 --> 01:42:06,291
we can change this behavior with two arguments, error-badlines and warn-badlines.

1295
01:42:06,291 --> 01:42:12,617
Both take Boolean or true-false values. Setting error-badlines to false makes pandas

1296
01:42:12,617 --> 01:42:18,190
skip badlines and load the rest of the data, instead of throwing an error.

1297
01:42:18,190 --> 01:42:23,739
Warn Bad Lines tells pandas whether to display messages when unparsable lines are

1298
01:42:23,739 --> 01:42:29,152
skipped. Let's try importing the corrupted file again, this time with Error Bad

1299
01:42:29,152 --> 01:42:34,838
Lines set to false and Warn Bad Lines equal to true. Success! A word of caution. If

1300
01:42:34,838 --> 01:42:40,456
lines were skipped, it's worth investigating what was left out to see if there are

1301
01:42:40,456 --> 01:42:44,430
underlying issues that should be addressed. In this video,

1302
01:42:44,430 --> 01:42:49,904
you've learned about common issues encountered importing data from flat files, and

1303
01:42:49,904 --> 01:42:55,044
how to handle those issues. Now, it's your turn to practice. Welcome! In this

1304
01:42:55,044 --> 01:43:00,585
chapter, you will learn how to build pipelines to data stored in spreadsheets, plus

1305
01:43:00,585 --> 01:43:05,925
additional data wrangling techniques. Spreadsheets, also called Excel files, are

1306
01:43:05,925 --> 01:43:11,332
ubiquitous today. If you ever worked with them, the data frame structure probably

1307
01:43:11,332 --> 01:43:12,400
looked familiar.

1308
01:43:12,400 --> 01:43:18,084
Like data frames, spreadsheets organize information in tables, with cells of data in

1309
01:43:18,084 --> 01:43:23,700
rows and columns. Unlike data frames and flat files, spreadsheets can have formulas

1310
01:43:23,700 --> 01:43:29,316
with automatically updating results. Spreadsheets also support formatting that flat

1311
01:43:29,316 --> 01:43:34,932
files do not. While data frames can be formatted for presentation purposes, like in

1312
01:43:34,932 --> 01:43:39,330
Jupyter Notebooks, Pandas does not import spreadsheet formatting.

1313
01:43:39,330 --> 01:43:44,539
so check that spreadsheets you want to load do not use formatting to convey

1314
01:43:44,539 --> 01:43:50,164
important information. Or better yet, export them without formatting. Finally, an

1315
01:43:50,164 --> 01:43:55,998
Excel file or workbook can have multiple spreadsheets. At its simplest, reading data

1316
01:43:55,998 --> 01:44:01,693
from a spreadsheet resembles reading a flat file, just with its own function. Read

1317
01:44:01,693 --> 01:44:07,249
Excel. Let's demonstrate with an Excel file of responses from FreeCodeCamp's New

1318
01:44:07,249 --> 01:44:08,430
Developer Survey.

1319
01:44:08,430 --> 01:44:14,336
which asks new developers about their employment, education, job goals, and learning

1320
01:44:14,336 --> 01:44:20,171
resources used. This file has been modified to make it easier to practice with, but

1321
01:44:20,171 --> 01:44:25,936
you can grab the full dataset from Kaggle or FreeCodeCamp's GitHub. Previewing the

1322
01:44:25,936 --> 01:44:31,842
file in a spreadsheet program, we see that it's pretty straightforward. Tabular data

1323
01:44:31,842 --> 01:44:36,060
with no formatting. As always, we first import Pandas as PD.

1324
01:44:36,060 --> 01:44:41,835
then pass the spreadsheet path as a string to pd.readExcel and assign the result to

1325
01:44:41,835 --> 01:44:47,679
a variable. When we check the head, the data frame looks as expected. Of course, not

1326
01:44:47,679 --> 01:44:53,454
all spreadsheets are so simple, and formatting features that help human readers can

1327
01:44:53,454 --> 01:44:58,880
make it trickier to read by machine. Spreadsheets commonly contain non-tabular

1328
01:44:58,880 --> 01:45:04,586
information, such as metadata headers, or have smaller tables of information, like

1329
01:45:04,586 --> 01:45:05,490
this invoice.

1330
01:45:05,490 --> 01:45:11,703
Fortunately, ReadExcel, like ReadCSV, has keyword arguments to select columns and

1331
01:45:11,703 --> 01:45:18,069
skip or limit rows to get only the cells you want. ReadExcel shares many parameters

1332
01:45:18,069 --> 01:45:24,436
with ReadCSV, but some work slightly differently, so check Panda's documentation if

1333
01:45:24,436 --> 01:45:30,572
you're unsure. nRows is the same, taking an integer of rows to load. ReadExcel's

1334
01:45:30,572 --> 01:45:33,180
skipRows argument is also similar.

1335
01:45:33,180 --> 01:45:39,417
It takes either a number of rows to skip at the beginning, or a list of row numbers

1336
01:45:39,417 --> 01:45:45,353
to skip. Finally, ReadExcel's useCalls argument accepts a list of either column

1337
01:45:45,353 --> 01:45:51,665
numbers or column names, or a function to decide whether to load a column, just like

1338
01:45:51,665 --> 01:45:57,677
ReadCSV's useCalls. However, it also accepts an integer of columns to load, or a

1339
01:45:57,677 --> 01:46:02,110
string of Excel column letters or ranges, like A through P.

1340
01:46:02,110 --> 01:46:07,729
Let's use these arguments to get a subset of survey response data focusing on

1341
01:46:07,729 --> 01:46:13,714
employment. The columns are organized alphabetically with job-related variables in

1342
01:46:13,714 --> 01:46:19,844
columns W through AB and income in AR. Also note that this version of the data has a

1343
01:46:19,844 --> 01:46:25,975
two-row header providing links to the source that we'll want to exclude. We load the

1344
01:46:25,975 --> 01:46:31,740
data with Read Excel as before, but set skip rows to 2 to exclude the metadata.

1345
01:46:31,740 --> 01:46:37,187
Since we know which column letters we want, we can pass the range and standalone

1346
01:46:37,187 --> 01:46:42,906
income column as a single string to use calls. Checking the data frame, we see there

1347
01:46:42,906 --> 01:46:48,353
are only a few columns loaded and no metadata. In this lesson, you learned about

1348
01:46:48,353 --> 01:46:53,936
creating data frames from Excel data. Now, it's your turn to practice. You can now

1349
01:46:53,936 --> 01:46:58,430
create data frames from a single spreadsheet or from parts of one.

1350
01:46:58,430 --> 01:47:03,899
It's fairly common for Excel files to contain multiple sheets of data though. In

1351
01:47:03,899 --> 01:47:09,231
this lesson, you'll learn how to specify sheets to get data from, as well as a

1352
01:47:09,231 --> 01:47:14,699
workflow to combine multiple sheets into a single data frame. Although the terms

1353
01:47:14,699 --> 01:47:20,236
spreadsheet and Excel file are often used interchangeably, an Excel file can be a

1354
01:47:20,236 --> 01:47:25,500
workbook with multiple sheets containing data as well as charts and metadata.

1355
01:47:25,500 --> 01:47:31,619
will focus on sheets with tabular data. By default, ReadXL pulls data from the first

1356
01:47:31,619 --> 01:47:37,593
sheet in a workbook only. This behavior can be changed with the sheet name keyword

1357
01:47:37,593 --> 01:47:43,493
argument. SheetName accepts the name of the desired sheet as a string, an integer

1358
01:47:43,493 --> 01:47:49,394
indicating the desired sheet's position in the workbook, or a list containing any

1359
01:47:49,394 --> 01:47:54,930
combination of sheet names and position numbers to read. Two things to note.

1360
01:47:54,930 --> 01:48:00,884
are that sheets are zero index, and that if you load multiple sheets at once, any

1361
01:48:00,884 --> 01:48:06,985
other arguments passed to ReadXL apply to all sheets. For example, if you set nRows

1362
01:48:06,985 --> 01:48:12,719
to 50, the first 50 rows of each sheet listed in sheet name will be loaded. If

1363
01:48:12,719 --> 01:48:18,526
sheets need different parameters, load them with separate ReadXL calls. The new

1364
01:48:18,526 --> 01:48:24,480
developer survey file actually contains two sheets, one with responses from 2016.

1365
01:48:24,480 --> 01:48:31,257
another with responses from 2017. We already got 2016's data since it's the first

1366
01:48:31,257 --> 01:48:38,117
sheet. Let's get 2017. To get the second sheet containing 2017's responses, we can

1367
01:48:38,117 --> 01:48:44,893
either pass its position index, 1, to sheet name, or the actual sheet name, 2017.

1368
01:48:44,893 --> 01:48:51,753
Either method gets the same results, as shown when we check if the two data frames

1369
01:48:51,753 --> 01:48:52,590
are equal.

1370
01:48:52,590 --> 01:48:58,136
What if we wanted to get all sheets in a workbook at once? We could list them all,

1371
01:48:58,136 --> 01:49:03,750
but the easier way is to pass none to sheet name. Let's do this for the survey data

1372
01:49:03,750 --> 01:49:09,228
and check the data type of the result. Instead of getting a data frame, we got an

1373
01:49:09,228 --> 01:49:14,909
ordered dictionary. Iterating through the items reveals that the keys are sheet name

1374
01:49:14,909 --> 01:49:20,320
and the values are data frames for each sheet. We have one data frame per sheet.

1375
01:49:20,320 --> 01:49:26,118
which is great if sheets have different columns or describe different subjects. For

1376
01:49:26,118 --> 01:49:31,776
the survey data, each sheet has the same columns, just for different years. Let's

1377
01:49:31,776 --> 01:49:37,015
combine them into one data frame. First, we create an empty data frame, all

1378
01:49:37,015 --> 01:49:42,464
responses. Then, we loop through the items in the survey responses dictionary.

1379
01:49:42,464 --> 01:49:48,332
Remember, each value is a data frame corresponding to a worksheet, and each key is a

1380
01:49:48,332 --> 01:49:49,100
sheet name.

1381
01:49:49,100 --> 01:49:54,651
For each data frame, we add a column, year, containing the sheet name, so we know

1382
01:49:54,651 --> 01:49:59,859
which data set a record came from. Finally, we append the data frames to all

1383
01:49:59,859 --> 01:50:05,068
responses. We can check unique values in the year column to confirm that all

1384
01:50:05,068 --> 01:50:10,550
responses has both years. In this case, we only combined two sheets, so we could

1385
01:50:10,550 --> 01:50:16,032
have appended one to the other, but looping has the advantage of working for any

1386
01:50:16,032 --> 01:50:17,540
number of sheets. Now,

1387
01:50:17,540 --> 01:50:22,909
it's your turn to practice working with multiple sheets. In this course, you've

1388
01:50:22,909 --> 01:50:28,414
mostly handled string and numeric data. This lesson focuses on another data type,

1389
01:50:28,414 --> 01:50:33,919
boolean, and special considerations for working with them. A boolean variable has

1390
01:50:33,919 --> 01:50:39,560
only two possible values, true or false, which makes them convenient for tasks like

1391
01:50:39,560 --> 01:50:43,570
filtering. Despite this simplicity, booleans can be tricky.

1392
01:50:43,570 --> 01:50:49,156
We'll use a subset of the new developer survey data to focus on them. This version

1393
01:50:49,156 --> 01:50:54,879
only has an ID column, and columns for whether the respondent attended a programming

1394
01:50:54,879 --> 01:51:00,465
boot camp, and if they took out a loan for it. True and false are represented in a

1395
01:51:00,465 --> 01:51:06,052
few ways for demonstration purposes. 0s and 1s, which are common among people with

1396
01:51:06,052 --> 01:51:11,434
coding experience, trues and falses, and yeses and nos, which tend to appear in

1397
01:51:11,434 --> 01:51:12,660
surveys and forms.

1398
01:51:12,660 --> 01:51:18,138
Let's load this data with no additional arguments and check D types. Pandas

1399
01:51:18,138 --> 01:51:24,055
interpreted no columns as Boolean. Even true-false columns were loaded as floats.

1400
01:51:24,055 --> 01:51:30,044
Let's investigate. First, let's sum the data frame's columns to see how many trues

1401
01:51:30,044 --> 01:51:36,034
each float column has. Recall that these columns code true as 1 and false as 0. In

1402
01:51:36,034 --> 01:51:41,220
our data subset, 38 attended a boot camp and 14 took out a loan for it.

1403
01:51:41,220 --> 01:51:46,824
Let's also check how many values in each column are missing by summing the results

1404
01:51:46,824 --> 01:51:52,222
of isNA. Every record has a value for bootcamp attendance, but most of the loan

1405
01:51:52,222 --> 01:51:57,758
values are blank, even for some students who attended a bootcamp. Now, let's cast

1406
01:51:57,758 --> 01:52:03,361
these columns as booleans with the dtype argument. ReadExcel's dtype works exactly

1407
01:52:03,361 --> 01:52:08,692
like ReadCSV's, so we can pass a dictionary specifying which columns should be

1408
01:52:08,692 --> 01:52:10,400
boolean. Checking dtypes,

1409
01:52:10,400 --> 01:52:16,136
It looks like it worked. Checking counts of true values reveals issues. The lone

1410
01:52:16,136 --> 01:52:22,159
columns have too many trues, and the yes-no ones are all true. Checking NA values by

1411
01:52:22,159 --> 01:52:27,465
column, we see there aren't any. What happened? Pandas automatically loads

1412
01:52:27,465 --> 01:52:33,201
true-false columns as floats, but that can be changed with dtype. Boolean values

1413
01:52:33,201 --> 01:52:37,360
must be either true or false, so NAs were recoded as true.

1414
01:52:37,360 --> 01:52:43,055
While pandas recognized that 0s and 1s are false and true respectively, it did not

1415
01:52:43,055 --> 01:52:48,680
know what to do with yes and no, so they were all coded as true. We can solve the

1416
01:52:48,680 --> 01:52:54,514
issue of yes-no columns being misinterpreted with read Excel's true values and false

1417
01:52:54,514 --> 01:53:00,070
values argument. Each takes a list of values that pandas should treat as true or

1418
01:53:00,070 --> 01:53:05,626
false when they appear in Boolean columns. Values in non-Boolean columns are not

1419
01:53:05,626 --> 01:53:06,320
converted.

1420
01:53:06,320 --> 01:53:12,248
Let's pass yes and no as single-item lists to true values and false values. Then, we

1421
01:53:12,248 --> 01:53:17,400
check true counts with the sum method. Now the yes-no columns match their

1422
01:53:17,400 --> 01:53:23,045
counterparts, but there's still the issue of NAs being coded as true. What to do

1423
01:53:23,045 --> 01:53:28,762
depends on the data. In our case, we don't want fake trues, so we might decide to

1424
01:53:28,762 --> 01:53:31,020
keep the lone columns as floats.

1425
01:53:31,020 --> 01:53:36,127
Things to consider when casting a column as Boolean include the presence of NA

1426
01:53:36,127 --> 01:53:41,495
values, how the column will be used in the analysis, the consequences of fake true

1427
01:53:41,495 --> 01:53:46,602
values, and whether alternative representations like floats would do. You just

1428
01:53:46,602 --> 01:53:51,578
learned how to cast columns as Boolean and set custom true and false values.

1429
01:53:51,578 --> 01:53:56,881
Importantly, you also learned what to consider before doing so. Now, it's time to

1430
01:53:56,881 --> 01:53:57,470
practice.

1431
01:53:57,470 --> 01:54:03,133
You've now worked with numeric, string, and boolean data. This lesson will focus on

1432
01:54:03,133 --> 01:54:08,524
one last data type you're likely to encounter. Date times. How computers handle

1433
01:54:08,524 --> 01:54:14,051
dates and times is a rich topic, but what you need to know for now is that Python

1434
01:54:14,051 --> 01:54:19,509
stores them as a special data type. Date time. Date times can be translated into

1435
01:54:19,509 --> 01:54:21,420
myriad text representations.

1436
01:54:21,420 --> 01:54:27,048
and there is a common set of codes used to describe how datetimes are formatted as

1437
01:54:27,048 --> 01:54:32,675
strings. By default, pandas loads datetime data as objects. If you want to arrange

1438
01:54:32,675 --> 01:54:38,235
records by time, select within a time span, or calculate intervals though, you'll

1439
01:54:38,235 --> 01:54:44,000
need datetime columns. We use the parseDates keyword argument, not dtype, to specify

1440
01:54:44,000 --> 01:54:49,490
datetime columns. parseDates accepts a list of column names or numbers to parse.

1441
01:54:49,490 --> 01:54:55,267
It also accepts a list of lists where each sublist is a group of columns that should

1442
01:54:55,267 --> 01:55:00,562
be combined and parsed as one, such as separate day, month, and year columns.

1443
01:55:00,562 --> 01:55:06,201
Finally, to combine columns, parse them, and store the result as a new column, you

1444
01:55:06,201 --> 01:55:11,977
can supply a dictionary where each key is a new column name and each value is a list

1445
01:55:11,977 --> 01:55:16,860
of columns to parse. Let's see this with the new developer survey data.

1446
01:55:16,860 --> 01:55:22,629
whose date-time columns have been modified for demonstration purposes. Part 1

1447
01:55:22,629 --> 01:55:28,848
start-time and Part 1 end-time have data in standard year, month, day, hour, second

1448
01:55:28,848 --> 01:55:34,991
format. Part 2 start-time's data has been split into date and time columns. Part 2

1449
01:55:34,991 --> 01:55:41,285
end-time is in a non-standard format. To parse the dates in standard format, we pass

1450
01:55:41,285 --> 01:55:46,080
the column names in a list to read Excel's parse dates argument.

1451
01:55:46,080 --> 01:55:52,145
When we check the dtypes of the timestamp column, we see the two columns were parsed

1452
01:55:52,145 --> 01:55:58,065
successfully. To parse the split up timestamp column, we can add a list within the

1453
01:55:58,065 --> 01:56:03,913
list containing part2 startDate and part2 startTime, and pass that to parseDates.

1454
01:56:03,913 --> 01:56:09,616
Pandas creates a new combined datetime column, part2 startDate underscore part2

1455
01:56:09,616 --> 01:56:14,670
startTime. But to control the column names, let's create a dictionary.

1456
01:56:14,670 --> 01:56:21,208
pass that instead, and view the resulting column. However, parseDates only works if

1457
01:56:21,208 --> 01:56:27,353
the data is in a format that pandas understands. If you try to parse unusually

1458
01:56:27,353 --> 01:56:33,812
formatted dates with parseDates, like 12-31-99 for December 31st, 1999, you'll get

1459
01:56:33,812 --> 01:56:40,272
the columns back as strings. Instead, convert non-standard dates after import with

1460
01:56:40,272 --> 01:56:42,320
pandas to datetime method.

1461
01:56:42,320 --> 01:56:48,047
ToDateTime takes the data frame and column to convert, plus a format argument

1462
01:56:48,047 --> 01:56:54,295
containing a string that describes how the data is formatted. DateTime formatting is

1463
01:56:54,295 --> 01:57:00,394
described with a set of codes. Sturftime.org is a valuable reference for them all.

1464
01:57:00,394 --> 01:57:06,196
Some important codes are the ones for four-digit year, zero padded month, zero

1465
01:57:06,196 --> 01:57:10,510
padded day, hour on the 24-hour clock, zero padded minute,

1466
01:57:10,510 --> 01:57:21,927
and zero padded second. Let's use toDateTime to parse part 2 end time. We review the

1467
01:57:21,927 --> 01:57:32,799
data to build the string format description, %m, %d, %y, %h, %m, %s. We pass the

1468
01:57:32,799 --> 01:57:38,100
data frame and column to pd toDateTime.

1469
01:57:38,100 --> 01:57:43,684
and supply the format string to the format keyword argument. We reassign the result

1470
01:57:43,684 --> 01:57:48,999
back to part 2 end time. When we check the column, we see the dates were parsed

1471
01:57:48,999 --> 01:57:54,650
correctly. That's a lot of ways to parse a date. Now, it's your turn to practice. In

1472
01:57:54,650 --> 01:57:59,763
this chapter, you'll learn to build pipelines to relational databases, which

1473
01:57:59,763 --> 01:58:04,876
underpin the information systems of many organizations. Relational databases

1474
01:58:04,876 --> 01:58:07,500
organize data about entities in tables.

1475
01:58:07,500 --> 01:58:13,077
with rows representing instances of entities and columns of attributes. This

1476
01:58:13,077 --> 01:58:18,948
probably sounds familiar. Data frames, flat files, and many Excel sheets arrange

1477
01:58:18,948 --> 01:58:25,040
data similarly. Relational databases differ in that tables can be linked or related

1478
01:58:25,040 --> 01:58:31,057
via unique record identifiers or keys. Databases handle more data and support more

1479
01:58:31,057 --> 01:58:34,800
simultaneous users than spreadsheets or flat files.

1480
01:58:34,800 --> 01:58:40,537
They also offer more data quality controls, like enforcing column data types, and we

1481
01:58:40,537 --> 01:58:46,273
interface with databases via a specific language, Structured Query Language, or SQL.

1482
01:58:46,273 --> 01:58:51,805
Common relational databases include Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL, and

1483
01:58:51,805 --> 01:58:57,337
SQLite, which this course uses. Unlike the others, SQLite databases are stored as

1484
01:58:57,337 --> 01:59:00,000
regular, self-contained computer files.

1485
01:59:00,000 --> 01:59:05,940
regular, self-contained computer files, just as CSVs and Excel files are, making

1486
01:59:05,940 --> 01:59:12,103
them great for sharing data. Reading data from a database is a two-step process. We

1487
01:59:12,103 --> 01:59:18,192
first make a way to connect to the database, then query it with SQL and Pandas. To

1488
01:59:18,192 --> 01:59:24,281
do this, we'll use the SQLAlchemy library, which has tools to work with many major

1489
01:59:24,281 --> 01:59:26,880
relational databases. Specifically,

1490
01:59:26,880 --> 01:59:32,905
we'll use SQLAlchemy's CreateEngine function. CreateEngine takes a string URL to a

1491
01:59:32,905 --> 01:59:39,004
database and makes an engine object that manages database connection. URLs follow a

1492
01:59:39,004 --> 01:59:45,029
pattern that varies slightly depending on the database. For SQLite, the pattern is

1493
01:59:45,029 --> 01:59:50,980
SQLite, colon, three forward slashes, then the database file name. Once we create

1494
01:59:50,980 --> 01:59:56,050
the database engine, we can pull data with Panda's read SQL function.

1495
01:59:56,050 --> 02:00:01,676
Read SQL needs two arguments. The first is a string of either a SQL query or, to

1496
02:00:01,676 --> 02:00:07,512
load a whole table, just the table name. The second argument is a way to connect to

1497
02:00:07,512 --> 02:00:13,208
the database. We'll supply the engine we made here. Let's take a minute to review

1498
02:00:13,208 --> 02:00:18,693
SQL SELECT statements, which are used to query databases. The basic syntax is,

1499
02:00:18,693 --> 02:00:24,107
select column names from table name. This will get all rows for the specified

1500
02:00:24,107 --> 02:00:24,670
columns.

1501
02:00:24,670 --> 02:00:30,492
To get all rows and all columns, use SELECT star from table name. A note about code

1502
02:00:30,492 --> 02:00:36,244
style. Keywords like SELECT and FROM are not case sensitive, but it's conventional

1503
02:00:36,244 --> 02:00:41,925
to type them in all capital letters. It's also best practice to mark the end of a

1504
02:00:41,925 --> 02:00:47,537
SQL statement with a semicolon. Now let's put all this together to fetch weather

1505
02:00:47,537 --> 02:00:53,218
data from a SQLite database containing information about New York City. We import

1506
02:00:53,218 --> 02:00:54,130
pandas as pd.

1507
02:00:54,130 --> 02:01:00,182
plus the createEngine function from SQLAlchemy. Then, we make the engine object,

1508
02:01:00,182 --> 02:01:06,008
passing the database URL string sqlite colon slash slash slash data.db to the

1509
02:01:06,008 --> 02:01:12,135
createEngine function. Since we want everything in the weather table, we can make

1510
02:01:12,135 --> 02:01:18,188
the first argument to read SQL the table name as a string, or we can use the SQL

1511
02:01:18,188 --> 02:01:22,500
statement select star from weather as the first argument.

1512
02:01:22,500 --> 02:01:27,864
We also supply the engine object as the second argument. Then we can check out our

1513
02:01:27,864 --> 02:01:33,358
new data frame. And that's the basic workflow to get data from a relational database

1514
02:01:33,358 --> 02:01:38,722
with Pandas. Now it's your turn to practice. Now that you know how to connect to a

1515
02:01:38,722 --> 02:01:44,020
database and query it with Pandas, let's focus on refining imports with different

1516
02:01:44,020 --> 02:01:49,318
SQL queries. Pandas read SQL function has fewer arguments than read Excel or read

1517
02:01:49,318 --> 02:01:49,580
CSV.

1518
02:01:49,580 --> 02:01:55,825
but SQL lets you customize data imports in even more ways. The last lesson mentioned

1519
02:01:55,825 --> 02:02:01,996
one such way. Specifying columns in a SELECT statement lets you load only variables

1520
02:02:01,996 --> 02:02:07,869
you are interested in studying, akin to use calls in ReadExcel and ReadCSV. The

1521
02:02:07,869 --> 02:02:13,742
statement SELECT DATE FROM WEATHER, for example, gets only the date and average

1522
02:02:13,742 --> 02:02:16,270
temperatures in the weather table.

1523
02:02:16,270 --> 02:02:22,606
The other common way to selectively get data with SQL is by using a WHERE clause to

1524
02:02:22,606 --> 02:02:28,332
filter rows. The syntax looks like, select columns from table where a given

1525
02:02:28,332 --> 02:02:34,592
condition or conditions are met. To import records based on numeric values, we can

1526
02:02:34,592 --> 02:02:40,928
use standard operators to compare numbers equals, greater than, and greater than or

1527
02:02:40,928 --> 02:02:45,890
equal to, less than, and less than or equal to, and not equal to.

1528
02:02:45,890 --> 02:02:51,749
Note that SQL's equality and inequality operators differ from Python. The weather

1529
02:02:51,749 --> 02:02:57,752
data contains Fahrenheit temperatures, so the SQL query to get all days with a high

1530
02:02:57,752 --> 02:03:03,828
temperature above freezing would be, select star from weather, where Tmax is greater

1531
02:03:03,828 --> 02:03:09,542
than 32. WHERE clauses can also filter text. To match a string exactly, use the

1532
02:03:09,542 --> 02:03:13,810
equal sign, followed by the text to match in single quotes.

1533
02:03:13,810 --> 02:03:19,884
Note that string matching is case sensitive. To get call records about incidents in

1534
02:03:19,884 --> 02:03:25,885
the borough of Brooklyn, for example, the query would be, select star from HPD 311

1535
02:03:25,885 --> 02:03:31,886
calls where borough equals Brooklyn, with Brooklyn in single quotes. Let's combine

1536
02:03:31,886 --> 02:03:37,887
SQL and Python to get Brooklyn calls. As before, we import pandas and SQLAlchemy's

1537
02:03:37,887 --> 02:03:39,570
create engine function.

1538
02:03:39,570 --> 02:03:45,122
We pass createEngine the URL to data.db and assign the result to engine. Since our

1539
02:03:45,122 --> 02:03:50,810
SQL queries are getting more complicated, we'll write out the query first and assign

1540
02:03:50,810 --> 02:03:56,159
it to the variable query. Wrapping the string in triple quotes lets us split it

1541
02:03:56,159 --> 02:04:01,779
between multiple lines so it's easier to read. Then we pass the query and engine to

1542
02:04:01,779 --> 02:04:07,060
read SQL. When we check the unique borough values in the resulting data frame,

1543
02:04:07,060 --> 02:04:13,477
we see there are only Brooklyn calls. We can even combine conditions with SQL's AND

1544
02:04:13,477 --> 02:04:19,431
and OR operators, AND returns only records where all conditions are true. For

1545
02:04:19,431 --> 02:04:25,925
example, if we create the query, select star from HPD 311 calls where borough equals

1546
02:04:25,925 --> 02:04:32,111
Bronx and complaint type equals plumbing, then pass the query and engine to read

1547
02:04:32,111 --> 02:04:32,420
SQL.

1548
02:04:32,420 --> 02:04:38,640
we get the 2016 call records about plumbing from the Bronx. The OR operator returns

1549
02:04:38,640 --> 02:04:44,861
records with at least one MET condition. If we change our query to get records that

1550
02:04:44,861 --> 02:04:50,407
are about plumbing OR waterly, we get 10,684 records that meet one or both

1551
02:04:50,407 --> 02:04:56,178
conditions. As you probably noticed, SQL is integral to building pipelines to

1552
02:04:56,178 --> 02:05:00,000
databases, even when you're working in Python. Now,

1553
02:05:00,000 --> 02:05:05,699
It's your turn to practice. The SQL we've used so far mimics the functionality of

1554
02:05:05,699 --> 02:05:11,469
pandas' keyword arguments like use calls and skip rows. In this lesson, we'll take

1555
02:05:11,469 --> 02:05:16,957
advantage of SQL's features to wrangle data in ways that cannot be done at the

1556
02:05:16,957 --> 02:05:22,727
import stage using pandas alone. In an analysis, you might need unique values in a

1557
02:05:22,727 --> 02:05:27,230
column, or unique combinations of values across several columns.

1558
02:05:27,230 --> 02:05:33,398
Examples include getting unique values to check data quality and creating crosswalks

1559
02:05:33,398 --> 02:05:39,565
between values to combine datasets. Alternatively, data might have duplicate records

1560
02:05:39,565 --> 02:05:45,586
that should be excluded. Pandas has tools to do this wrangling in data frames, but

1561
02:05:45,586 --> 02:05:51,534
it can be done with SQL during import using SELECT DISTINCT. The syntax is SELECT

1562
02:05:51,534 --> 02:05:53,810
DISTINCT COLUMNAMES FROM TABLE.

1563
02:05:53,810 --> 02:06:00,000
To remove duplicate records, select distinct on all columns with the query. Select

1564
02:06:00,000 --> 02:06:06,114
distinct star from table. For another example, if we wanted to map buildings with

1565
02:06:06,114 --> 02:06:11,927
housing complaints, we could use the query, select distinct incident address,

1566
02:06:11,927 --> 02:06:17,815
borough, from HPD 311 calls to get unique street addresses and boroughs. Other

1567
02:06:17,815 --> 02:06:23,250
times, you might not be interested in the details of individual records.

1568
02:06:23,250 --> 02:06:28,929
particularly when visualizing data. You can query the database directly for

1569
02:06:28,929 --> 02:06:34,987
descriptive statistics with aggregate functions like SUM, AVERAGE, MAX, MIN, and

1570
02:06:34,987 --> 02:06:41,121
COUNT. The first four functions all take a single column name in parentheses. For

1571
02:06:41,121 --> 02:06:47,028
example, the query SELECT AVERAGE TMAX FROM WEATHER, with TMAX in parentheses,

1572
02:06:47,028 --> 02:06:52,480
returns the average daily high temperature. COUNT is a little different.

1573
02:06:52,480 --> 02:06:57,932
while it can accept a single column name. You can do things like get the number of

1574
02:06:57,932 --> 02:07:03,451
rows that fit a query with count star, or even get the number of unique values in a

1575
02:07:03,451 --> 02:07:08,970
column with count distinct and the column name. Aggregate functions return a single

1576
02:07:08,970 --> 02:07:14,356
number on their own. More likely, you want data summarized by categories, such as

1577
02:07:14,356 --> 02:07:19,875
average high temperatures by month, or counts of plumbing complaints by borough. In

1578
02:07:19,875 --> 02:07:20,540
that case,

1579
02:07:20,540 --> 02:07:26,137
Add a group by clause after the select statement and where clauses. Remember to

1580
02:07:26,137 --> 02:07:31,948
select the column you're grouping by as well as the aggregate function. Otherwise,

1581
02:07:31,948 --> 02:07:37,899
you'll end up with unlabeled summary figures for each group. The query to get counts

1582
02:07:37,899 --> 02:07:43,568
of plumbing complaints by burrow, for example, would be select burrow count star

1583
02:07:43,568 --> 02:07:48,740
from HPD 311 calls where complaint type equals plumbing. Group by burrow.

1584
02:07:48,740 --> 02:07:53,944
Let's see what the results of that query look like in a data frame. With the

1585
02:07:53,944 --> 02:07:59,559
necessary libraries imported, we create the engine, write out the query, then pass

1586
02:07:59,559 --> 02:08:05,311
the query and engine to Panda's read SQL function. When we check the results, we see

1587
02:08:05,311 --> 02:08:10,721
we have a neat summary data frame ready for plotting. This has been a SQL-heavy

1588
02:08:10,721 --> 02:08:16,199
lesson, with Python as the glue connecting SQL queries to data visualization and

1589
02:08:16,199 --> 02:08:17,500
analysis libraries.

1590
02:08:17,500 --> 02:08:23,353
Working in two languages at once is tricky at first, but it's a useful skill, and it

1591
02:08:23,353 --> 02:08:29,206
gets easier. One feature of relational databases is that tables can be linked to one

1592
02:08:29,206 --> 02:08:34,850
another via unique record identifiers, or keys. This lets users combine tables to

1593
02:08:34,850 --> 02:08:40,424
custom datasets with SQL join, which we'll explore in this lesson. As mentioned,

1594
02:08:40,424 --> 02:08:45,650
records typically have keys that uniquely identify them. At their simplest,

1595
02:08:45,650 --> 02:08:51,563
keys can be automatically assigned row numbers, like in the 311 call data, but they

1596
02:08:51,563 --> 02:08:57,406
can also carry meaning, like university course numbers. When records include other

1597
02:08:57,406 --> 02:09:03,248
tables' keys, you can bring in or join data from the reference table. For example,

1598
02:09:03,248 --> 02:09:09,090
given a column of instructor ID numbers and a professor table, we can then join in

1599
02:09:09,090 --> 02:09:12,510
professors' names. When building data pipelines,

1600
02:09:12,510 --> 02:09:18,170
This means you're not limited to working with a single table's column. Let's join

1601
02:09:18,170 --> 02:09:23,761
weather data to 311 call records to study if certain problems are exacerbated by

1602
02:09:23,761 --> 02:09:29,421
weather conditions. Both tables contain date columns as text with unique dates in

1603
02:09:29,421 --> 02:09:35,082
the weather table, so they'll be the join key. First, we select star from HPD 311

1604
02:09:35,082 --> 02:09:40,882
calls to get all columns there. Star will also get all columns from weather when we

1605
02:09:40,882 --> 02:09:41,860
join it. Then,

1606
02:09:41,860 --> 02:09:48,059
we join weather on HPD311Calls.createdDate equals weather.date, which are the key

1607
02:09:48,059 --> 02:09:54,334
column. We use dot notation to specify the table and column when querying multiple

1608
02:09:54,334 --> 02:10:00,457
tables. Two things to note here. Join, by default, only returns records with key

1609
02:10:00,457 --> 02:10:06,656
values that appear in both tables, and key columns must be the same data type, or

1610
02:10:06,656 --> 02:10:08,110
nothing will match.

1611
02:10:08,110 --> 02:10:13,686
We can incorporate a WHERE clause after the join to refine the dataset. Here, we

1612
02:10:13,686 --> 02:10:19,402
filter the data to focus on heat and hot water calls, which probably spike in cold

1613
02:10:19,402 --> 02:10:24,909
weather. It's even possible to summarize data and then join additional columns.

1614
02:10:24,909 --> 02:10:30,276
Imagine we wanted to compare call counts by borough against census data about

1615
02:10:30,276 --> 02:10:35,922
population and housing in another table, borough census. Let's build the query in

1616
02:10:35,922 --> 02:10:36,340
parts.

1617
02:10:36,340 --> 02:10:43,715
We first get call counts by borough by selecting hpd311calls.borough and count star

1618
02:10:43,715 --> 02:10:51,089
from hpd311calls, grouping by hpd311calls.borough. Because both tables have borough

1619
02:10:51,089 --> 02:10:54,910
columns, we have to specify the table here.

1620
02:10:54,910 --> 02:11:00,386
The borough census table has more columns than we need, so we also select its total

1621
02:11:00,386 --> 02:11:05,598
population and housing units columns. Notice that we don't have to list borough

1622
02:11:05,598 --> 02:11:10,810
census in the from clause. Finally, we add the join clause between the from and

1623
02:11:10,810 --> 02:11:15,957
group by clauses, joining on the borough column. Let's pass this query and the

1624
02:11:15,957 --> 02:11:21,103
database engine to read SQL and check out the results. Looks like the Bronx is

1625
02:11:21,103 --> 02:11:23,280
overrepresented in our call data.

1626
02:11:23,280 --> 02:11:28,311
As you can tell, SQL queries can get complicated. Let's review the order of

1627
02:11:28,311 --> 02:11:33,812
keywords. First comes the SELECT statement, including aggregate functions, and the

1628
02:11:33,812 --> 02:11:39,179
table you're selecting from. Then a JOIN clause, if there is one. Then the WHERE

1629
02:11:39,179 --> 02:11:44,545
clause, if any. Last comes GROUP BY. This is just the beginning when it comes to

1630
02:11:44,545 --> 02:11:50,046
using SQL plus Pandas to build data pipelines. But enough of me talking about SQL.

1631
02:11:50,046 --> 02:11:51,790
It's your turn. Until now.

1632
02:11:51,790 --> 02:11:57,141
you've built pipelines to data in tabular formats. In this chapter, we'll shift our

1633
02:11:57,141 --> 02:12:02,557
focus to data in JavaScript Object Notation, or JSON data. As the name implies, JSON

1634
02:12:02,557 --> 02:12:07,909
is based on JavaScript, but popular programming languages have versions of the data

1635
02:12:07,909 --> 02:12:13,002
structures JSONs use, making them easy for programs to generate and parse while

1636
02:12:13,002 --> 02:12:18,160
still being human-readable. And because of JavaScript's role in web development,

1637
02:12:18,160 --> 02:12:23,809
JSON is a common format for transmitting data through the web. Unlike data frames,

1638
02:12:23,809 --> 02:12:29,320
JSON data is not tabular. This makes for more efficient data storage. If a value

1639
02:12:29,320 --> 02:12:35,038
doesn't exist for a record, the attribute can be omitted instead of storing it with

1640
02:12:35,038 --> 02:12:40,273
a null value. In other words, records don't all have to have the same set of

1641
02:12:40,273 --> 02:12:45,922
attributes. Instead, data is organized to collections of objects. Objects resemble

1642
02:12:45,922 --> 02:12:47,300
Python dictionaries.

1643
02:12:47,300 --> 02:12:53,538
They're enclosed in curly braces and contain attribute value pairs. One last feature

1644
02:12:53,538 --> 02:12:59,553
of JSONs is that they can be nested. Values themselves can be objects or lists of

1645
02:12:59,553 --> 02:13:05,642
objects. You can guess the panda's function to load a JSON into a data frame. It's

1646
02:13:05,642 --> 02:13:11,806
readJSON. ReadJSON takes a string of the path to the JSON to load. This path can be

1647
02:13:11,806 --> 02:13:15,890
to a file saved on a computer or a URL ending in .json.

1648
02:13:15,890 --> 02:13:21,721
Alternatively, you can supply JSON data directly as a string. As with flat files and

1649
02:13:21,721 --> 02:13:27,414
spreadsheets, Pandas guesses attribute data types, but you can specify them with a

1650
02:13:27,414 --> 02:13:33,175
dictionary of column names and values and the dtype argument. JSON data can be laid

1651
02:13:33,175 --> 02:13:39,006
out in various ways, so there is an orient keyword argument to flag uncommon layout.

1652
02:13:39,006 --> 02:13:44,560
Since JSON data isn't tabular, Pandas makes assumptions about how it's arranged.

1653
02:13:44,560 --> 02:13:49,938
or oriented to load it into a data frame, Pandas automatically detects record and

1654
02:13:49,938 --> 02:13:55,183
column orientation, which you'll encounter most often. Let's see what they look

1655
02:13:55,183 --> 02:14:00,760
like. A record-oriented JSON consists of a list of dictionaries, each translating to

1656
02:14:00,760 --> 02:14:06,270
a table record. For example, this JSON of causes of death from New York City's open

1657
02:14:06,270 --> 02:14:11,582
data site is record-oriented. To reduce the file size by not repeating attribute

1658
02:14:11,582 --> 02:14:11,980
names,

1659
02:14:11,980 --> 02:14:17,533
a JSON may be column oriented. The entire JSON is a dictionary. Keys are column

1660
02:14:17,533 --> 02:14:23,437
name. Values are lists of values for that column, or dictionaries of row indices and

1661
02:14:23,437 --> 02:14:29,131
column values, like in this rearranged version of the death causes data. However,

1662
02:14:29,131 --> 02:14:34,894
other orientations are possible. This version of the death data is split oriented,

1663
02:14:34,894 --> 02:14:40,728
with different lists for column names, indices, and values. Let's load it to a data

1664
02:14:40,728 --> 02:14:41,150
frame.

1665
02:14:41,150 --> 02:14:46,499
We import pandas, then load the data with readjson, specifying orientation by

1666
02:14:46,499 --> 02:14:52,057
passing one of a set of documented string values to the orient keyword argument.

1667
02:14:52,057 --> 02:14:57,407
When we print the data, it looks like it loaded correctly. And that's a quick

1668
02:14:57,407 --> 02:15:03,243
introduction to JSON. We'll dive deeper in later lessons. But now, it's your turn to

1669
02:15:03,243 --> 02:15:08,870
practice working. You now know how to load JSON data into pandas, given a path or

1670
02:15:08,870 --> 02:15:10,190
URL to a JSON file.

1671
02:15:10,190 --> 02:15:15,914
In this lesson, we'll turn our attention to working with Web Application Programming

1672
02:15:15,914 --> 02:15:21,639
Interfaces, or APIs, the most common source of JSON data. An application programming

1673
02:15:21,639 --> 02:15:27,159
interface is a defined way for an application to communicate with other programs,

1674
02:15:27,159 --> 02:15:32,815
and vice versa. They let programmers get data from an application without having to

1675
02:15:32,815 --> 02:15:38,267
know about that application's database architecture. One caveat, APIs are shared

1676
02:15:38,267 --> 02:15:38,880
resources

1677
02:15:38,880 --> 02:15:44,424
and often limit how much data you can get in a specified time frame. Using an API to

1678
02:15:44,424 --> 02:15:49,441
get data is like using a catalog to order products. The catalog shows what's

1679
02:15:49,441 --> 02:15:54,853
available and provides order instructions. You send a properly formed order to the

1680
02:15:54,853 --> 02:15:59,869
right address and get back what you asked for. Similarly, an API provides an

1681
02:15:59,869 --> 02:16:05,414
endpoint to send requests to, and documentation describes what a request should look

1682
02:16:05,414 --> 02:16:07,790
like, such as parameters to include.

1683
02:16:07,790 --> 02:16:14,010
While there are Python libraries geared towards popular APIs, we'll use the requests

1684
02:16:14,010 --> 02:16:20,155
library in this course. Requests lets users send and get data from any URL, so it's

1685
02:16:20,155 --> 02:16:26,375
not tied to any particular API. The function to retrieve data from a URL, logically,

1686
02:16:26,375 --> 02:16:31,780
is requests get. Requests get takes a string of the URL to get data from.

1687
02:16:31,780 --> 02:16:37,404
and has optional keyword arguments that are useful for working with APIs. The params

1688
02:16:37,404 --> 02:16:42,827
keyword lets you pass a dictionary of parameter names and values to customize API

1689
02:16:42,827 --> 02:16:48,251
requests. The headers keyword also takes a dictionary of names and values. If the

1690
02:16:48,251 --> 02:16:53,540
API you're using requires a user authentication key, it should be passed in the

1691
02:16:53,540 --> 02:16:58,160
header. The result is a response object containing data and metadata.

1692
02:16:58,160 --> 02:17:03,978
we need to use the response's json method to get just the data. Importantly, the

1693
02:17:03,978 --> 02:17:09,942
json method returns a dictionary, which readjson can't parse. It expects a string.

1694
02:17:09,942 --> 02:17:15,833
To load the data to a data frame, we need to use pd data frame instead. We'll use

1695
02:17:15,833 --> 02:17:21,942
requests to work with data from Yelp. Yelp lets users submit ratings and reviews for

1696
02:17:21,942 --> 02:17:27,760
businesses and makes that data available via its APIs. In the API documentation,

1697
02:17:27,760 --> 02:17:33,610
We see the endpoint URL, optional and required parameters to use in a request, and

1698
02:17:33,610 --> 02:17:39,461
even a sample response. Note that if we want business details, the data we need is

1699
02:17:39,461 --> 02:17:45,453
nested under the businesses key. Let's get data from the API about bookstores in San

1700
02:17:45,453 --> 02:17:51,090
Francisco, California. We import pandas and the requests library, then create a

1701
02:17:51,090 --> 02:17:53,230
variable for the API endpoint.

1702
02:17:53,230 --> 02:17:59,023
The API documentation indicated required parameters and authorization, so we also

1703
02:17:59,023 --> 02:18:04,316
set up a dictionary of request parameter fields and values, plus a headers

1704
02:18:04,316 --> 02:18:10,253
dictionary of authorization info. API keys are strings used to identify the program

1705
02:18:10,253 --> 02:18:16,189
calling the API and confirm it can make the call. They're sensitive information, so

1706
02:18:16,189 --> 02:18:20,910
it's hidden behind the API key variable here. We pass the API URL.

1707
02:18:20,910 --> 02:18:26,447
params, and headers to requests get and store the result as response. Since

1708
02:18:26,447 --> 02:18:32,354
responses contain data and metadata, we use the JSON method to isolate the data.

1709
02:18:32,354 --> 02:18:38,187
Let's check it before loading it to a data frame. We have a dictionary with the

1710
02:18:38,187 --> 02:18:44,241
information we want in a list under the businesses key. We pass data businesses to

1711
02:18:44,241 --> 02:18:50,000
