02:39:37,760 --> 02:39:43,455
but it's helpful to know what magic commands are at your disposal. Consider this

1958
02:39:43,455 --> 02:39:49,434
example. We want to inspect the runtime for selecting 1,000 random numbers between 0

1959
02:39:49,434 --> 02:39:55,058
and 1 using NumPy's random.rand function. Using timeit just requires adding the

1960
02:39:55,058 --> 02:40:00,681
magic command before the line of code we want to analyze. That's it. One simple

1961
02:40:00,681 --> 02:40:03,600
command to gather runtimes. Magic indeed.

1962
02:40:03,600 --> 02:40:09,164
One advantage to using TimeIt is the fact that it provides an average of timing

1963
02:40:09,164 --> 02:40:14,940
statistics. Notice that the output provides a mean and standard deviation of time.

