2058
02:48:34,811 --> 02:48:35,280
similar

2059
02:48:35,280 --> 02:48:42,107
to the line profiler package. It can be downloaded via pip and comes with a handy

2060
02:48:42,107 --> 02:48:48,766
magic command, mprun, that uses the same syntax as lprun. One drawback to using

2061
02:48:48,766 --> 02:48:55,846
mprun is that any function profiled for memory consumption must be defined in a file

2062
02:48:55,846 --> 02:49:02,841
and imported. mprun can only be used on functions defined in physical files and not

2063
02:49:02,841 --> 02:49:04,780
in the IPython session.

2064
02:49:04,780 --> 02:49:11,986
In this example, the convertUnits function was placed in a file named herofunks.py.

2065
02:49:11,986 --> 02:49:19,279
Now, we simply import our function from the herofunks file and use the magic command

2066
02:49:19,279 --> 02:49:26,572
MPRun to gather statistics on its memory footprint. MPRun output is similar to LPRun

2067
02:49:26,572 --> 02:49:32,910
output. Here, we see a line-by-line description of the memory consumption

2068
02:49:32,910 --> 02:49:38,782
for the function in question. The first column represents the line number of the

