03:40:53,098 --> 03:40:57,200
with statements are perfectly legal this code opens the source file

2698
03:40:57,200 --> 03:41:02,077
and then opens the destination file inside the source file's context. That means

2699
03:41:02,077 --> 03:41:07,197
code that runs inside the context created by opening the destination file has access

2700
03:41:07,197 --> 03:41:12,135
to both the f underscore source and the f underscore dest file objects. So we are

2701
03:41:12,135 --> 03:41:17,195
able to copy the file over one line at a time like we wanted to. One thing you will

2702
03:41:17,195 --> 03:41:21,889
want to think about when writing your context managers is what happens if the

2703
03:41:21,889 --> 03:41:26,400
programmer who uses your context manager writes code that causes an error?

2704
03:41:26,400 --> 03:41:31,234
Imagine you've written this function that lets someone connect to the printer. The

2705
03:41:31,234 --> 03:41:36,126
printer only allows one connection at a time, so it is imperative that p.disconnect

2706
03:41:36,126 --> 03:41:41,019
gets called, or else no one else will be able to print. Someone decides to use your

2707
03:41:41,019 --> 03:41:45,676
git printer function to print the text of their document. However, they weren't

2708
03:41:45,676 --> 03:41:50,509
paying attention and accidentally typed txt instead of text. This will raise a key

2709
03:41:50,509 --> 03:41:53,280
error because txt is not in the doc dictionary.

2710
03:41:53,280 --> 03:41:57,715
and that means p disconnect doesn't get called. So what can we do? You may be

2711
03:41:57,715 --> 03:42:02,324
