6746
08:44:23,368 --> 08:44:27,600
acronym implies that there is a sense of direction between the nodes.

6747
08:44:27,600 --> 08:44:32,916
The arrows on the edges indicate the direction. The acyclic part simply means that

6748
08:44:32,916 --> 08:44:38,296
when you traverse the directed graph, there is no way for you to circle back to the

6749
08:44:38,296 --> 08:44:43,676
same node. In Airflow, the nodes are operators, each instance of which can be given

6750
08:44:43,676 --> 08:44:48,862
a unique label, the task ID. Operators do something, like run a Python script or

6751
08:44:48,862 --> 08:44:54,308
schedule tasks to the cloud provider. They're triggered by a scheduler, but executed

6752
08:44:54,308 --> 08:44:55,280
by an executor.

6753
08:44:55,280 --> 08:45:00,361
which is typically a different process. In code, an Airflow DAC requires a DAC ID.

6754
08:45:00,361 --> 08:45:05,505
Some of the most common optional arguments are the schedule interval, where you can

6755
08:45:05,505 --> 08:45:10,400
pass a cron schedule, and the date on which the DAC should start. Operators are

6756
08:45:10,400 --> 08:45:15,544
assigned to this DAC later in the code. Tasks are essentially instances of operator

6757
08:45:15,544 --> 08:45:20,315
classes. Airflow comes with many, but you can also define your own operators.

6758
08:45:20,315 --> 08:45:22,980
They're typically named after what they do.

6759
08:45:22,980 --> 08:45:28,785
a bash operator runs a bash command a python operator runs a python script and so on

6760
08:45:28,785 --> 08:45:34,244
dependencies between operators are coded with a set upstream and set downstream

6761
08:45:34,244 --> 08:45:39,911
methods like this you could also define the same relationships using the bit shift

