09:05:56,560 --> 09:06:02,221
Use the client's delete object method, passing the bucket name and object key to

7002
09:06:02,221 --> 09:06:07,670
delete the object. In this lesson, we learn that buckets are like folders and

7003
09:06:07,670 --> 09:06:13,472
objects are like files within them. We learn to create the client before we can do

7004
09:06:13,472 --> 09:06:19,204
anything else. We learn how to upload files to a bucket, how to list objects in a

7005
09:06:19,204 --> 09:06:24,865
bucket, how to head object or get object metadata, how to download a file from a

7006
09:06:24,865 --> 09:06:25,360
bucket,

7007
09:06:25,360 --> 09:06:30,818
And finally, how to delete an object. Let's help Sam continue working on her

7008
09:06:30,818 --> 09:06:36,707
pipeline. In the previous chapter, we learned how to create and delete buckets and

7009
09:06:36,707 --> 09:06:42,739
upload objects inside of them. Often, we work with private data or data we want only

7010
09:06:42,739 --> 09:06:48,772
certain users to see. This is where the AWS permission system comes in. AWS defaults

7011
09:06:48,772 --> 09:06:53,440
to denying permission. That means if we upload a file by default,

7012
09:06:53,440 --> 09:06:59,042
only our key and secret have access to it. This behavior may sound annoying, but

7013
09:06:59,042 --> 09:07:04,784
it's much more secure to opt in to allowing public access to our data than to have

7014
09:07:04,784 --> 09:07:10,176
it be public by default. If we or our colleague tries to download a file with

7015
09:07:10,176 --> 09:07:15,988
pandas, S3 won't let them, even if it's in the same script that uploaded. But if we

7016
09:07:15,988 --> 09:07:21,520
initialize the S3 client with our credentials, it will work. Let's learn how to

7017
09:07:21,520 --> 09:07:22,780
protect and expose

7018
09:07:22,780 --> 09:07:29,549
are buckets and objects. There are four ways we can control permissions in S3. We

7019
09:07:29,549 --> 09:07:36,486
can use IAM to control users' access to AWS services, buckets, and objects. We used

7020
09:07:36,486 --> 09:07:42,921
it in lesson one to give permissions by attaching IAM policies to a user. IAM

7021
09:07:42,921 --> 09:07:49,774
applies across all AWS services. Bucket policies give us control on the bucket and

7022
09:07:49,774 --> 09:07:52,030
the objects within it. ACLs

7023
09:07:52,030 --> 09:07:58,896
or access control lists let us set permissions on specific objects within a bucket.

7024
09:07:58,896 --> 09:08:05,845
Lastly, pre-signed URLs let us provide temporary access to an object. IAM and bucket

7025
09:08:05,845 --> 09:08:12,711
policies are great in multi-user environments, but since SAM isn't managing one, we

7026
09:08:12,711 --> 09:08:19,660
will focus on ACLs and pre-signed URLs. ACLs are entities attached to objects in S3.

7027
09:08:19,660 --> 09:08:26,752
We will focus on two types of ACL, private and public read. Say we upload a file. By

7028
09:08:26,752 --> 09:08:33,590
default, its ACL is private. Let's set the ACL to public read using S3 put object

7029
09:08:33,590 --> 09:08:40,597
ACL method. Now anyone in the world can download this file. We can also set the ACL

7030
09:08:40,597 --> 09:08:47,520
as public read on upload. We pass a dictionary with key ACL and value public read.

7031
09:08:47,520 --> 09:08:55,063
to the extra args parameter. Once we have a public object, anyone in the world can

7032
09:08:55,063 --> 09:09:02,698
access it at the URL within the format of bucket.s3.amazonaws.com slash object key.

7033
09:09:02,698 --> 09:09:10,057
A URL for an object with the key 2019 slash potholes.csv and bucket GAD requests

7034
09:09:10,057 --> 09:09:16,220
will look like this. We can use Python's nifty string format method

7035
09:09:16,220 --> 09:09:22,062
to generate a public URL for an object in S3. This is different than a pre-signed

7036
09:09:22,062 --> 09:09:27,831
URL, which we will cover in the next lesson. We create a string where the bucket

7037
09:09:27,831 --> 09:09:33,384
name and the object key are empty bracket. We call the format method, passing

7038
09:09:33,384 --> 09:09:39,298
positional arguments of bucket, GID request, and key, 2019 slash potholes dot CSV.

7039
09:09:39,298 --> 09:09:44,130
We can now pass this URL to something like pandas with no problems.

7040
09:09:44,130 --> 09:09:50,054
So how does this all work together? When a request comes in, if it's a pre-signed

7041
09:09:50,054 --> 09:09:56,125
URL, it will allow the download. If it's not pre-signed, it will check the policies

7042
09:09:56,125 --> 09:10:02,122
to make sure they allow the download. AWS's default behavior is to deny access. In

7043
09:10:02,122 --> 09:10:08,266
this lesson, we learned about AWS permission system. IAM answers, what can this user

7044
09:10:08,266 --> 09:10:13,020
do in AWS? Bucket policies answer, who can access this S3 bucket?

7045
09:10:13,020 --> 09:10:20,482
ACLs answer who can access this object and pre-signed URLs let us grant temporary

7046
09:10:20,482 --> 09:10:28,219
access to objects. We can set ACLs to public read or private on existing objects, or

7047
09:10:28,219 --> 09:10:35,865
we can upload with a specified ACL using extra args. Lastly, we can generate public

7048
09:10:35,865 --> 09:10:39,550
URLs for objects with a public read ACL.

7049
09:10:39,550 --> 09:10:45,406
Now that we have a grasp of basic AWS permissions, in the last lesson, we learned

7050
09:10:45,406 --> 09:10:51,190
