7672
10:04:56,784 --> 10:04:57,460
PostgreSQL.

7673
10:04:57,460 --> 10:05:02,511
There are basic data types for numbers, such as BigInt, or strings of characters,

7674
10:05:02,511 --> 10:05:07,438
such as character varying. There are also more high-level data types like CIDR,

7675
10:05:07,438 --> 10:05:12,302
which can be used for IP addresses. Implementing such a type on a column would

7676
10:05:12,302 --> 10:05:17,478
disallow anything that doesn't fit the structure of an IP. Data types also restrict

7677
10:05:17,478 --> 10:05:20,160
possible SQL operations on the stored data.

7678
10:05:20,160 --> 10:05:25,660
for example it is impossible to calculate the product from an integer and the text

7679
10:05:25,660 --> 10:05:31,293
column as shown here in the example the text column wind speed may store numbers but

7680
10:05:31,293 --> 10:05:36,860
postgres sql doesn't know how to use text in a calculation the solution for this is

7681
10:05:36,860 --> 10:05:38,000
typecasts that is

7682
10:05:38,000 --> 10:05:43,078
on the fly type conversion in this case you can use the cast function followed by

7683
10:05:43,078 --> 10:05:48,344
the column name the ask keyword and the desired data type and postgres sql will turn

7684
10:05:48,344 --> 10:05:53,360
win speed into an integer right before the calculation okay let's look into this

7685
10:05:53,360 --> 10:05:58,375
first working with data types is straightforward in a database management system

7686
10:05:58,375 --> 10:06:03,203
like postgres sql as said before data types are attribute constraints and are

7687
10:06:03,203 --> 10:06:06,400
therefore implemented for single columns of a table

7688
10:06:06,400 --> 10:06:11,544
they define the so-called domain of values in a column that means what form these

7689
10:06:11,544 --> 10:06:16,879
values can take and what not therefore they also define what operations are possible

7690
10:06:16,879 --> 10:06:22,213
with the values in the column as you saw in the previous exercises of course through

7691
10:06:22,213 --> 10:06:27,231
this consistent storage is enforced so a street number will always be an actual

7692
10:06:27,231 --> 10:06:32,502
number and a postal code will always have no more than six digits according to your

7693
10:06:32,502 --> 10:06:35,550
conventions this greatly helps with data quality

7694
10:06:35,550 --> 10:06:40,862
Here are the most common types in PostgreSQL. Note that these types are specific to

7695
10:06:40,862 --> 10:06:46,111
PostgreSQL, but appear in many other database management systems as well, and they

7696
10:06:46,111 --> 10:06:51,295
mostly conform to the SQL standard. The text type allows character strings of any

7697
10:06:51,295 --> 10:06:56,608
length, while the varchar and char types specify a maximum number of characters, or

7698
10:06:56,608 --> 10:07:01,664
a character string of fixed length, respectively. You'll use these two for your

7699
10:07:01,664 --> 10:07:02,240
database.

7700
10:07:02,240 --> 10:07:07,519
the boolean type allows for two boolean values for example true and false or one or

7701
10:07:07,519 --> 10:07:12,607
zero and for a third unknown value expressed through null then there are various

7702
10:07:12,607 --> 10:07:17,568
formats for date and time calculation also with time zone support numeric is a

7703
10:07:17,568 --> 10:07:22,784
general type for any sort of numbers with arbitrary precision while integer allows

7704
10:07:22,784 --> 10:07:27,999
only whole numbers in a certain range if that range is not enough for your numbers

