10048
13:10:10,159 --> 13:10:14,425
cleaning, data types, and schemas. Let's move on to some further sport concepts,

10049
13:10:14,425 --> 13:10:18,584
immutability and lazy processing. Normally in Python and most other languages,

10050
13:10:18,584 --> 13:10:22,956
variables are fully mutable. The values can be changed at any given time, assuming

10051
13:10:22,956 --> 13:10:27,382
the scope of the variable is valid. While very flexible, this does present problems

10052
13:10:27,382 --> 13:10:31,647
anytime there are multiple concurrent components trying to modify the same data.

10053
13:10:31,647 --> 13:10:33,780
Most languages work around these issues.

10054
13:10:33,780 --> 13:10:38,474
using constructs like mutexes, semaphores, etc. This can add complexity, especially

10055
13:10:38,474 --> 13:10:43,054
with non-trivial programs. Unlike typical Python variables, Spark data frames are

10056
13:10:43,054 --> 13:10:47,352
immutable. While not strictly required, immutability is often a component of

10057
13:10:47,352 --> 13:10:51,536
functional programming. We won't go into everything that implies here, but

10058
13:10:51,536 --> 13:10:56,230
understand that Spark is designed to use immutable objects. Practically, this means

10059
13:10:56,230 --> 13:11:00,867
Spark data frames are defined once and are not modifiable after initialization. If

10060
13:11:00,867 --> 13:11:02,450
the variable name is reused,

10061
13:11:02,450 --> 13:11:06,807
the original data is removed, assuming it's not in use elsewhere, and the variable

10062
13:11:06,807 --> 13:11:11,269
name is reassigned to the new data. While this seems inefficient, it actually allows

10063
13:11:11,269 --> 13:11:15,732
Spark to share data between all cluster components. It can do so without worry about

10064
13:11:15,732 --> 13:11:20,142
concurrent data objects. This is a quick example of the immutability of data frames

10065
13:11:20,142 --> 13:11:20,620
in Spark.

