10794
14:03:38,770 --> 14:03:43,780
Caching is keeping data in memory so that it does not have to be refetched or

10795
14:03:43,780 --> 14:03:48,855
recalculated each time it is used. Using caching properly is an important best

10796
14:03:48,855 --> 14:03:54,256
practice to know when working with Spark. Spark is aggressive about freeing up data

10797
14:03:54,256 --> 14:03:59,526
from memory. It will error on the side of unloading data from memory if it is not

10798
14:03:59,526 --> 14:04:04,797
used, even if it is going to be needed later. Eviction policy determines when and

10799
14:04:04,797 --> 14:04:08,180
which data is removed from cache. The policy is LRU.

10800
14:04:08,180 --> 14:04:15,057
least recently used. Each worker manages its own cache, and eviction depends on the

10801
14:04:15,057 --> 14:04:21,934
memory available to each worker. To cache a dataframe, use df.cache. To uncache it,

10802
14:04:21,934 --> 14:04:28,479
use df.onpersist. To determine whether a dataframe is cached, use the dataframe

10803
14:04:28,479 --> 14:04:35,273
property is underscore cached. df.is cached confirmed that it was not cached. Then

10804
14:04:35,273 --> 14:04:36,350
we cached it.

10805
14:04:36,350 --> 14:04:43,430
Using df.isCached again confirms that it was cached. You uncache a data frame using

10806
14:04:43,430 --> 14:04:50,595
Unpersist. A data frame storage level specifies five details about how it is cached.

10807
14:04:50,595 --> 14:04:57,761
Use disk, use memory, use off heap, deserialized, and replication. We can set any or

10808
14:04:57,761 --> 14:05:03,220
all of these. However, the cache operation sets them to default.

10809
14:05:03,220 --> 14:05:08,826
Use disk specifies whether to spill the data frame to disk if it falls out of

10810
14:05:08,826 --> 14:05:14,870
memory. Use memory specifies whether to keep the data in memory. Use off-heap tells

10811
14:05:14,870 --> 14:05:20,476
Spark to use off-heap storage instead of on-heap memory. Even though the best

10812
14:05:20,476 --> 14:05:26,593
performance is obtained when operating solely in on-heap memory, Spark also makes it

10813
14:05:26,593 --> 14:05:30,670
possible to use off-heap storage for certain operations.

10814
14:05:30,670 --> 14:05:36,509
off-heap storage is slightly slower than on-heap but still faster than disk. The

10815
14:05:36,509 --> 14:05:42,493
downside is that the user has to manually deal with managing the allocated memory.

10816
14:05:42,493 --> 14:05:48,551
Deserialized true is faster but uses more memory. Deserialized or rather serialized

10817
14:05:48,551 --> 14:05:54,609
data is more space efficient but slower to read than deserialized data. This option

10818
14:05:54,609 --> 14:05:57,090
only applies to in-memory storage.

10819
14:05:57,090 --> 14:06:04,504
disk cache is always serialized. Replication is used to tell Spark to replicate data

10820
14:06:04,504 --> 14:06:11,478
on multiple nodes. This allows faster fault recovery when a node fails. You may

10821
14:06:11,478 --> 14:06:18,627
wonder why you use df.cache to cache, but df.unpersist to uncache. That's because

10822
14:06:18,627 --> 14:06:25,600
df.cache is shorthand for df.persist with the first argument set to its default

10823
14:06:25,600 --> 14:06:26,130
value.

10824
14:06:26,130 --> 14:06:31,616
The persist command allows you to specify the desired storage level using the first

10825
14:06:31,616 --> 14:06:37,037
argument. If that argument is not provided, it uses a default setting. When memory

10826
14:06:37,037 --> 14:06:42,259
is scarce, it is recommended to use memory and disk caching strategy. This will

10827
14:06:42,259 --> 14:06:47,613
spill the data frame to disk if memory runs low. Reading the data frame from disk

10828
14:06:47,613 --> 14:06:53,166
cache is slower than reading it from memory, but can still be faster than recreating

10829
14:06:53,166 --> 14:06:54,620
it from scratch. Cache

10830
14:06:54,620 --> 14:06:59,397
is equivalent to using Persist with the default storage level.

10831
