15:26:57,956 --> 15:27:03,184
Microsoft suggests using the throw statement in the new applications, but it is

11777
15:27:03,184 --> 15:27:08,610
still common to see the raise error statement in our current applications. This is

11778
15:27:08,610 --> 15:27:14,102
the raise error syntax. It starts with the word raise error. Notice it only has one

11779
15:27:14,102 --> 15:27:14,830
e, not two.

11780
15:27:14,830 --> 15:27:20,436
The first parameter can be a message string, a message ID or a variable that

11781
15:27:20,436 --> 15:27:26,263
contains the message string. The second parameter is the severity and the third

11782
15:27:26,263 --> 15:27:32,460
parameter is the state. After these parameters you can optionally add arguments like

11783
15:27:32,460 --> 15:27:38,656
strings or numbers. If the message string has some parameter placeholders such as %s

11784
15:27:38,656 --> 15:27:41,680
or %d, these arguments will replace them.

11785
15:27:41,680 --> 15:27:47,528
We will explain it in detail in the next slides. Finally, you can specify additional

11786
15:27:47,528 --> 15:27:53,375
options. These options won't be covered in this course. So, for further information,

11787
15:27:53,375 --> 15:27:58,596
you can have a look at Microsoft documentation. Let's see an example of the

11788
15:27:58,596 --> 15:28:04,235
RAISEERROR statement with message string. In the first line, we check if there is

11789
15:28:04,235 --> 15:28:09,734
any staff member whose ID is 15. In the recent, we invoke RAISEERROR with three

11790
15:28:09,734 --> 15:28:10,500
parameters.

11791
15:28:10,500 --> 15:28:16,545
The first parameter is the message text, no staff member with such ID. The second

11792
15:28:16,545 --> 15:28:22,591
one, 16, is the severity level. Finally, the last one is the state. If we execute

11793
15:28:22,591 --> 15:28:28,711
this example as there is no staff member whose ID is 15, we will get the following

11794
15:28:28,711 --> 15:28:34,980
output. If we don't specify an error number, the error number will always be 50,000.

11795
15:28:34,980 --> 15:28:40,842
Let's change the message text included on the previous raceError statement and add

11796
15:28:40,842 --> 15:28:46,704
some parameter placeholders. Instead of no staff member with such id, let's change

11797
15:28:46,704 --> 15:28:52,495
the words staff member by the percent symbol followed by the letter s. Let's also

11798
15:28:52,495 --> 15:28:58,429
include %d. %s is used for stream values and it will be replaced by the penultimate

11799
15:28:58,429 --> 15:29:01,360
parameter of this function, staff member.

11800
15:29:01,360 --> 15:29:07,043
%d is used for integer values and it will be replaced by the last parameter of this

11801
15:29:07,043 --> 15:29:12,588
function, 15. If we execute this example, we will get this output. If you want to

11802
15:29:12,588 --> 15:29:18,271
include the %character in your message, you will have to write it twice. Apart from

11803
15:29:18,271 --> 15:29:23,680
the letters s and d, the % symbol can be followed by other characters for other

11804
15:29:23,680 --> 15:29:29,020
purposes. You can deepen your knowledge by looking at Microsoft documentation.

11805
15:29:29,020 --> 15:29:34,560
In the raceError statement, we can also specify an error number instead of a message

11806
15:29:34,560 --> 15:29:40,033
string. This error number comes from sys.messages. We will learn more about the use

11807
15:29:40,033 --> 15:29:45,573
of sys.messages in the lesson dedicated to the formatMessage function. Let's include

11808
15:29:45,573 --> 15:29:50,783
the raceError statement inside a try-cut construct to see what happens. In this

11809
15:29:50,783 --> 15:29:54,740
example, the severity level of the raceError statement is 9.

11810
15:29:54,740 --> 15:29:59,628
If you remember from the previous chapter, severity levels lower than 11 are not

11811
15:29:59,628 --> 15:30:04,394
catchable by the catch block. So here, this error won't be caught by the catch

11812
15:30:04,394 --> 15:30:09,466
block. This will be the output. However, if we set up the severity level between 11

11813
15:30:09,466 --> 15:30:14,476
and 19, the error will be caught by the catch block. In this example, the severity

11814
15:30:14,476 --> 15:30:19,120
level is 16, so the output will be you are in the catch block. Now, you will

11815
15:30:19,120 --> 15:30:21,870
practice applying the raise errors technique.

11816
15:30:21,870 --> 15:30:26,533
Now that you have learned the raise error statement, let's discover the throw

11817
15:30:26,533 --> 15:30:31,499
statement. As we said in the previous lesson, Microsoft recommends using the throw

11818
15:30:31,499 --> 15:30:36,587
statement over the raise error statement. Let's see the throw syntax. It starts with

11819
15:30:36,587 --> 15:30:41,432
the word throw and can optionally be followed by a list of parameters. The first

11820
15:30:41,432 --> 15:30:46,337
parameter is the error number, the second parameter is the message, and the third

11821
15:30:46,337 --> 15:30:47,730
parameter is the state.

11822
15:30:47,730 --> 15:30:52,911
Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement allows re-throwing an original

11823
15:30:52,911 --> 15:30:58,030
error caught by a catch block. Let's see some examples. The throw statement can be

11824
15:30:58,030 --> 15:31:03,149
invoked without parameters within a catch block. In this example, we intentionally

11825
15:31:03,149 --> 15:31:08,143
created an error, dividing the price of the orders by zero. This error makes the

11826
15:31:08,143 --> 15:31:13,200
control pass to the catch block, invoking the throw statement without parameters.

11827
15:31:13,200 --> 15:31:18,468
The throw statement re-throws the original error caused in the try block and stops

11828
15:31:18,468 --> 15:31:23,672
the execution, so this selected statement, this line is executed, isn't executed.

11829
15:31:23,672 --> 15:31:28,876
The re-thrown error has the same error number, severity and state as the original

11830
15:31:28,876 --> 15:31:34,080
one. You have to be careful when using a statement before the throw statement. In

11831
15:31:34,080 --> 15:31:39,413
such a case, this statement must always end with a semicolon. Otherwise, SQL Server

11832
15:31:39,413 --> 15:31:41,340
will have unexpected behavior.

11833
15:31:41,340 --> 15:31:46,602
In this example, the SELECT statement that goes before the THROW statement doesn't

11834
15:31:46,602 --> 15:31:51,736
end with a semicolon. SQL Server thinks that the word THROW is an alias for this

11835
15:31:51,736 --> 15:31:57,063
SELECT statement. So, if we execute this example, we will get the following output.

11836
15:31:57,063 --> 15:32:00,400
We can see that the THROW statement wasn't executed.

11837
15:32:00,400 --> 15:32:05,508
Let's correct this script and add a semicolon after the SELECT statement before the

11838
15:32:05,508 --> 15:32:10,000
THROW statement. If we execute this code, we will see the text, this line

11839
15:32:10,000 --> 15:32:15,016
is executed with no column name, as it doesn't have an alias. Now the throw

11840
15:32:15,016 --> 15:32:20,031
statement is executed, so we get the information about the error. The throw

11841
15:32:20,031 --> 15:32:25,582
statement can also be used with parameters for the error number, message and state.

11842
15:32:25,582 --> 15:32:30,731
This syntax can be included within a catch block or outside of it. Here is an

11843
15:32:30,731 --> 15:32:36,282
example of the use of the throw statement. The first parameter, 52000, is the error

11844
15:32:36,282 --> 15:32:36,750
number.

11845
15:32:36,750 --> 15:32:42,364
This number can be greater than or equal to 50,000. The second parameter, this is an

11846
15:32:42,364 --> 15:32:47,845
example, is the message. Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement does

11847
15:32:47,845 --> 15:32:53,460
not allow the inclusion of parameter placeholders such as %d or %s. However, we will

11848
15:32:53,460 --> 15:32:58,940
learn how to do something similar in the next lesson. Finally, the third parameter

11849
15:32:58,940 --> 15:33:04,020
is the state, one in this example. This code generates the following output.

11850
15:33:04,020 --> 15:33:09,089
The throw statement doesn't allow the specification of the severity. SQL Server

11851
15:33:09,089 --> 15:33:14,350
always sets it to 16. You can include the throw statement with parameters within a

11852
15:33:14,350 --> 15:33:19,290
try block. In this example, as the recent any staff whose ID is 15, the throw

11853
15:33:19,290 --> 15:33:24,616
statement with parameters is invoked. As SQL Server always sets the severity of the

11854
15:33:24,616 --> 15:33:29,556
throw statement to 16, the control passes to the cat block, showing the error

11855
15:33:29,556 --> 15:33:33,470
message. Now, you will practice applying the throw statement.

11856
15:33:33,470 --> 15:33:38,463
In this lesson, we will learn how to customize error messages in the throw

11857
15:33:38,463 --> 15:33:43,928
statement. As we learned in a previous lesson, the throw statement does not allow

11858
15:33:43,928 --> 15:33:49,460
parameter placeholders such as %s or %d as the eraseError statement does. However,

11859
15:33:49,460 --> 15:33:55,128
there are two ways to customize our errors for the throw statement. The first one is

11860
15:33:55,128 --> 15:34:00,188
by using a variable and concatenate strings. The second one is by using the

11861
15:34:00,188 --> 15:34:01,740
formatMessage function.

11862
15:34:01,740 --> 15:34:06,768
Let's see how to use a variable to do it. In this example, we declared two

11863
15:34:06,768 --> 15:34:12,272
variables. Staff ID with a value of 500 and myMessage. With the use of the concat

11864
15:34:12,272 --> 15:34:17,776
function, we concatenate in the second variable, myMessage, the text. There is no

11865
15:34:17,776 --> 15:34:23,415
staff member for ID. With the value of the variable, staff ID, and finally with the

11866
15:34:23,415 --> 15:34:25,250
text, try with another one.

11867
15:34:25,250 --> 15:34:31,175
After that, the code takes, if it exists, any staff member whose ID has the value of

11868
15:34:31,175 --> 15:34:36,960
the variable staff ID. If the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing

11869
15:34:36,960 --> 15:34:42,885
the variable myMessage as a message parameter. If we execute this code as the recent

11870
15:34:42,885 --> 15:34:48,810
any staff member with that staff ID, we have the following output. There is no staff

11871
15:34:48,810 --> 15:34:50,080
member for ID 500.

11872
15:34:50,080 --> 15:34:55,285
Try with another one. The second way of customizing an error message is by using the

11873
15:34:55,285 --> 15:35:00,428
format message function. This is the syntax. It starts with the word format message

11874
15:35:00,428 --> 15:35:05,633
followed by a message string or a message number. After that, you can optionally add

11875
15:35:05,633 --> 15:35:10,652
arguments to replace the parameter placeholders of the first parameter. Let's see

11876
15:35:10,652 --> 15:35:14,990
how to use the format message function with the message string option.

11877
15:35:14,990 --> 15:35:20,314
In this example, we assigned the result of the formatMessage function to the

11878
15:35:20,314 --> 15:35:26,128
variable myMessage. Note that in the first parameter of the formatMessage function,

11879
15:35:26,128 --> 15:35:32,012
we included the parameter placeholders %d and %s. The value of the variable staff ID

11880
15:35:32,012 --> 15:35:37,685
will replace %d, and the text tried with another one will replace %s. After that,

11881
15:35:37,685 --> 15:35:43,219
the code also checks if there is any staff member whose ID has the value of the

11882
15:35:43,219 --> 15:35:44,480
variable staff ID.

11883
15:35:44,480 --> 15:35:49,864
If the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing the variable myMessage

11884
15:35:49,864 --> 15:35:55,183
as the message parameter. If we execute this code, as the recent any staff member

11885
15:35:55,183 --> 15:36:00,370
with that staff ID, we have the following output. Now, let's see how to use the

11886
15:36:00,370 --> 15:36:05,558
format message function with a message number. The possible message numbers are

11887
15:36:05,558 --> 15:36:10,548
cataloged in the sys.messages view. It contains a row for each message ID or

11888
15:36:10,548 --> 15:36:13,700
language ID of the error messages in the system.

11889
15:36:13,700 --> 15:36:20,057
We can choose any message ID or add a new message to this view to customize our

11890
15:36:20,057 --> 15:36:26,414
errors. To add a new message to sys.messages, we need to do it by executing the

11891
15:36:26,414 --> 15:36:32,931
spAddMessageStored procedure. The first three parameters of this stored procedure

11892
15:36:32,931 --> 15:36:39,369
are mandatory. The number of the message ID must be greater than 50,000. You can

11893
15:36:39,369 --> 15:36:43,070
optionally specify the language. If you don't,

11894
15:36:43,070 --> 15:36:49,137
it will be the default language of the session. In this course, we are not covering

11895
15:36:49,137 --> 15:36:55,276
the rest of the parameters. Let's add a new message to sys.messages by executing the

11896
15:36:55,276 --> 15:37:00,904
stored procedure spAddMessage. In this example, the message ID is 55,000, the

11897
15:37:00,904 --> 15:37:06,898
severity 16, the message text, there is no staff member for ID %d %s, and finally,

11898
15:37:06,898 --> 15:37:09,310
the language is American English.

11899
15:37:09,310 --> 15:37:15,090
Now that we have the message, we can use it in the format message function, passing

11900
15:37:15,090 --> 15:37:20,521
the number 55000 as the first parameter. In this lesson, you will learn what a

11901
15:37:20,521 --> 15:37:26,370
transaction is and how to combine transactions with error handling. We will practice

11902
15:37:26,370 --> 15:37:32,150
concepts related to transactions with a dataset based on bank account transactions.

11903
15:37:32,150 --> 15:37:37,790
The dataset contains information about customers, accounts, and transactions. So,

11904
15:37:37,790 --> 15:37:43,967
What is a transaction? A transaction is the execution of one or more statements such

11905
15:37:43,967 --> 15:37:49,777
that either all or none of these statements are executed. Let's see an example.

11906
15:37:49,777 --> 15:37:55,587
Imagine we want to transfer $100 from account A to account B. First, we have to

11907
15:37:55,587 --> 15:38:01,470
subtract $100 from account A and after that, add those $100 to account B. If the

11908
15:38:01,470 --> 15:38:06,030
second operation fails, we can't subtract $100 from account A.

11909
15:38:06,030 --> 15:38:11,459
These two operations must behave as an atomic operation. We execute both of them or

11910
15:38:11,459 --> 15:38:16,561
none of them. They need to be in the same transaction. SQL Server defines some

11911
15:38:16,561 --> 15:38:21,990
transaction statements. Begin tran or transaction is used to set the starting point

11912
15:38:21,990 --> 15:38:27,223
of a transaction. You can optionally add a name for the transaction and the with

11913
15:38:27,223 --> 15:38:31,540
mark option. These options won't be covered in this course. Commit

11914
15:38:31,540 --> 15:38:36,225
Optionally followed by tran or transaction marks the end of a successful

11915
15:38:36,225 --> 15:38:41,496
transaction. When executed, the effect of the transaction cannot be reversed. You

11916
15:38:41,496 --> 15:38:46,962
can optionally add the name of the transaction and set to on or off the with delayed

11917
15:38:46,962 --> 15:38:52,298
durability option. These options won't be covered in this course. Rollback tran or

11918
15:38:52,298 --> 15:38:57,699
transaction reverts a transaction to the beginning of it or a save point inside the

11919
15:38:57,699 --> 15:38:58,480
transaction.

11920
15:38:58,480 --> 15:39:04,715
After rollback tran or transaction, you can optionally add the transaction or the

11921
15:39:04,715 --> 15:39:11,104
save point name. We will define save points in the next lesson. Let's see all these

11922
15:39:11,104 --> 15:39:17,493
statements. In this example, we try to transfer $100 from account 1 with $24,400 to

11923
15:39:17,493 --> 15:39:23,420
account 5 with $35,300. We mark the start of the transaction with begin tran.

11924
15:39:23,420 --> 15:39:29,352
The first operation updates account 1, subtracting $100. Then, we register that

11925
15:39:29,352 --> 15:39:35,584
movement into the transactions table. After that, we add $100 to account 5 and then

11926
15:39:35,584 --> 15:39:41,891
register the movement into the transactions table. Finally, commit tran confirms the

11927
15:39:41,891 --> 15:39:47,898
changes. By executing this code, we can see that account 1 decreases by $100 and

11928
15:39:47,898 --> 15:39:50,000
account 2 increases by $100.

11929
15:39:50,000 --> 15:39:55,004
Besides, two rows are inserted into the transactions table. Let's change the

11930
15:39:55,004 --> 15:40:00,074
previous example using rollback tran instead of commit tran to revert all the

11931
15:40:00,074 --> 15:40:05,407
changes as if nothing had happened. Account 1 and 5 have the same balance as they

11932
15:40:05,407 --> 15:40:10,542
had before the execution of the script. Besides, no rows are inserted into the

11933
15:40:10,542 --> 15:40:16,007
transactions table. Let's surround the previous code with a try-catch. We start the

11934
15:40:16,007 --> 15:40:18,180
transaction within the try-block.

11935
15:40:18,180 --> 15:40:23,434
If everything between begin tran and commit tran executed without errors, the

11936
15:40:23,434 --> 15:40:29,098
changes will be confirmed. However, if there is an error, the catch block will take

11937
15:40:29,098 --> 15:40:34,762
the control and revert the changes with the rollback tran statement. Executing this

11938
15:40:34,762 --> 15:40:40,494
code, as there are no errors, account 1 decreases by $100 and account 2 increases by

11939
15:40:40,494 --> 15:40:44,930
$100. Besides, two rows are inserted into the transactions table.

11940
15:40:44,930 --> 15:40:50,257
Let's introduce an error in the previous script inserting $100 into the transactions

11941
15:40:50,257 --> 15:40:55,521
table for account 500, which doesn't exist. If we execute this code as there is one

11942
15:40:55,521 --> 15:41:00,277
error, the catch block will take the control, executing the rollback trance

11943
15:41:00,277 --> 15:41:05,604
statement. All the changes are reverted as if nothing had happened. Accounts 1 and 5

11944
15:41:05,604 --> 15:41:09,980
have the same balance as they had before the execution of the script.

11945
15:41:09,980 --> 15:41:15,711
Finally, let's see what happens if we don't explicitly specify a transaction. SQL

11946
15:41:15,711 --> 15:41:21,159
Server considers each statement as an individual transaction. The three first

11947
15:41:21,159 --> 15:41:27,103
statements are executed correctly, but the last statement fails, and this row is not

11948
15:41:27,103 --> 15:41:33,046
inserted. Account 1 decreases by $100 and account 2 increases by $100. However, only

11949
15:41:33,046 --> 15:41:38,990
one row is inserted into the transactions table, resulting in an inconsistent state.

11950
15:41:38,990 --> 15:41:44,953
In this lesson you will understand TRANCOUNT and save points. TRANCOUNT returns the

11951
15:41:44,953 --> 15:41:50,557
number of BEGIN TRAN statements that are active in your current connection. It

11952
15:41:50,557 --> 15:41:56,376
returns a value greater than 0 if you are in an open transaction and 0 if you are

11953
15:41:56,376 --> 15:42:02,195
not. It can be modified by the BEGIN TRAN statement which increases the TRANCOUNT

11954
15:42:02,195 --> 15:42:07,440
value by 1. The COMMIT TRAN statement decreases the TRANCOUNT value by 1.

11955
15:42:07,440 --> 15:42:13,028
The rollback tran statement decrements the tran count to zero except if there is a

11956
15:42:13,028 --> 15:42:18,412
save point. Let's see an example of tran count in a nested transaction. In this

11957
15:42:18,412 --> 15:42:24,068
example, we can see a nested transaction. There is a begin tran after another begin

11958
15:42:24,068 --> 15:42:29,656
tran before commit tran and rollback tran. Initially, the tran count value is zero

11959
15:42:29,656 --> 15:42:35,380
as there is no open transaction yet. After that, a begin tran statement is executed.

11960
15:42:35,380 --> 15:42:41,463
incrementing the tranCount value by one. Then, a delete statement deletes every row

11961
15:42:41,463 --> 15:42:47,472
from the transactions table. After that, another transaction is open, incrementing

11962
15:42:47,472 --> 15:42:53,628
the value of tranCount by one. The next statement deletes every row from the account

11963
15:42:53,628 --> 15:42:59,124
table. Then, a commit tran statement is executed, decrementing the value of

11964
15:42:59,124 --> 15:43:00,370
tranCount by one.

11965
15:43:00,370 --> 15:43:05,846
If the value of tranCount is 1 before executing the commit tran statement, the

11966
15:43:05,846 --> 15:43:11,603
current transaction is closed and the changes are confirmed. However, if its value

11967
15:43:11,603 --> 15:43:17,149
is richer than 1, like in this example where it is 2, the commit tran statement

11968
15:43:17,149 --> 15:43:22,414
decrements tranCount by 1 but doesn't commit the deletion of the table. The

11969
15:43:22,414 --> 15:43:24,380
transaction is still active.

11970
15:43:24,380 --> 15:43:29,866
Finally, rollback tran decreases the value of tranCount to 0 and reverts all the

11971
15:43:29,866 --> 15:43:35,626
uncommitted statements, including the deletion of the account table. Let's check the

11972
15:43:35,626 --> 15:43:41,043
content of transactions and accounts. We see that rows of these tables were not

11973
15:43:41,043 --> 15:43:46,529
deleted. Let's change the previous example using commit tran instead of rollback

11974
15:43:46,529 --> 15:43:48,860
tran in the penultimate statement.

11975
15:43:48,860 --> 15:43:54,576
The last commit tran statement will be executed when the tranCount value is 1, so it

11976
15:43:54,576 --> 15:44:00,225
will commit the deletion of the tables. If we check the content of transactions and

11977
15:44:00,225 --> 15:44:05,329
accounts, we can see that the rows of these tables were deleted. We can use

11978
15:44:05,329 --> 15:44:10,705
tranCount in a tricat construct to determine whether any transactions are still

11979
15:44:10,705 --> 15:44:14,720
open, by checking if the tranCount value is greater than 0.

11980
15:44:14,720 --> 15:44:20,215
If there are any open transactions, we can commit or roll back the changes. Let's

11981
15:44:20,215 --> 15:44:25,643
study save points. Save points are markers we can set within a transaction. They

11982
15:44:25,643 --> 15:44:31,138
allow us to roll back to the different save points we set. Save points start with

11983
15:44:31,138 --> 15:44:36,566
the word Savetran or transaction followed by the name. Let's see an example that

11984
15:44:36,566 --> 15:44:42,062
uses save points. This code opens a new transaction and after that marks the save

11985
15:44:42,062 --> 15:44:42,740
point one.

11986
15:44:42,740 --> 15:44:48,469
Then, the data of a new customer is inserted. Next, another save point, save point

11987
15:44:48,469 --> 15:44:54,337
2, is marked and the data of another customer is inserted. After that, rollback tran

11988
15:44:54,337 --> 15:45:00,065
